# Handoff Rules

## Sending Handoffs
- Write a draft handoff file with structured headers, then run
  `swarm_handoff.sh <draft-file>`. A `note` may include the bounded detail body
  described below; `git_handoff` remains headers-only.
- Use only these message types:
  - `git_handoff`
  - `note`
- Do not send `note` handoffs unless the user, role prompt, or constitution
  explicitly directs you to send one.
- When blocked by ambiguity, contradiction, or test/specification conflict,
  stop and ask for clarification; do not send a `note` handoff unless one of
  the explicit authorities above directed that note.
- For `git_handoff`, commit first, then write:

```text
type: git_handoff
to: <role>[,<role>...]
priority: NN
task: <short-stable-task-name>
commit: <10-character-commit-abbrev>
base: <10-character-base-commit-abbrev>
readiness: review-ready|qa-ready|release-candidate|final-ready
verified: review-ready|qa-candidate|<pack-id>[,<pack-id>...]|not-required
```

- When your role is an intermediate step in the pack pipeline, always forward
  a `git_handoff` to the next role in the chain after completing the inbound
  task, regardless of what changed. Formatting-only, manifest-only, audit-only,
  generated metadata, and other non-functional churn still require a forward
  down the chain.
- The preceding forwarding rule applies only to completed work. A functional
  task that fails the shared functional-delivery gate is incomplete and must
  not be forwarded downstream as a completed `git_handoff`.
- When a role prompt directs returning a functional-delivery failure, send the
  directed terse `note` to the named upstream role, complete the failed review,
  and do not forward the task farther down the pipeline.
- When your role sends the end-of-chain handoff to multiple recipients, those
  recipients inspect and merge the accepted commit, then close the current
  handoff with `done_with_current.sh`. They do not forward that handoff further.
  Only this terminal broadcast is merge-only without re-forwarding.
- Do not send an end-of-chain completion handoff for a functional task until
  the terminal functional-delivery gate confirms relevant production changes
  in the task lineage.
- Preserve the received task name when forwarding work for the same task. If the
  handoff starts new work, invent a short stable task name.
- For `note`, write:

```text
type: note
to: <role>[,<role>...]
priority: NN
message: <one line, max 80 chars>

<optional consolidated details, max 4000 chars>
```

- Keep `message` as a terse routing summary. Use the optional body only when an
  explicit authority requires consolidated findings that cannot fit truthfully in
  the summary. Prefer a short bullet list naming exact blockers, paths, failed
  checks, and skipped checks with their dependencies.
- If `swarm_handoff.sh` reports validation errors, repair the draft and rerun it.
- After a successful send, the helper removes the draft file. If you need to
  remove a stale draft manually, use `rm <draft-file>`, not `rm -f`.
- Do not add a body to `git_handoff`. Do not use a note body for verification
  narrative, sender process history, or information available from the referenced
  commit.
- Do not send tmux notifications directly.
- A delegated authority decision is not an ordinary handoff. Only the registered
  issuer may write a structured `type: unblocker` draft and submit it with
  `unblocker_send.sh`. The dedicated helper requires one recipient, priority 00,
  immutable authority and commit, exact task and active handoff, resume or
  exactly-bound replace mode, a stable superseded wait, and a bounded message.
  Free-form detail explains the decision but cannot enlarge the grant.
- `base:` identifies the received commit on which the candidate was based. For
  the first handoff in a task, use the starting repository commit from before the
  work. Preserve it only while forwarding an unchanged candidate; a new candidate
  commit uses the commit from which that role began its changes.
- `readiness: review-ready` with `verified: review-ready` is accepted only from coder to refactorer or refactorer to architect when the commit has matching focused evidence recorded by `node scripts/settled-final-verification.mjs record-review <receipt> <base> <task>`. It cannot be used for integration or completion.
- `readiness: qa-ready` with `verified: review-ready` is accepted only from architect to specifier with matching focused evidence for the exact candidate. It authorizes a fast-forward into `qa` only and is not final regression proof, master integration, or completion.
- `readiness: release-candidate` with `verified: qa-candidate` is accepted only from specifier to architect after the user explicitly requests master integration. Its candidate is the frozen QA head and its base is current `master`. It authorizes the architect's cumulative review and one terminal gate, not integration.
- A release-candidate handoff is rejected until the append-only granularity portfolio gives every applicable observation an explicit selected, combined, carried, or retired disposition and every selected hardening refinement has focused review-ready proof integrated into the exact QA ancestry. The terminal checkpoint cannot supply or erase those dispositions.
- `readiness: final-ready` is accepted for an architect-to-specifier master-promotion handoff only when `verified:` names all 20 canonical packs and the commit has matching
  durable evidence in `refs/notes/swarmforge-verification` for that exact base,
  task, and pack set. After committing, run the settled whole-pack checkpoint with
  `--property --changed-since <base> --prepare-evidence <task>`, then run
  `node scripts/verification-evidence.mjs record <printed-pending-file>` after the
  checkpoint process exits.
- Use `verified: not-required` only for documentation, specification, or prompt-only
  forwarding. The helper checks every canonical changed path and rejects mixed
  product, test, registry, build, or executable-tooling ranges. Coder handoffs
  cannot use it.
- The VTD-015 task itself omits `readiness:` and uses the previously integrated exact-pack protocol through integration. This bootstrap exception cannot be used by another task.
- Do not hand-edit, merge, stage, or commit handoff runtime state.

## Receiving Handoffs
- When notified, run `ready_for_next.sh`.
- `ready_for_next.sh` dispatches to the task or batch helper configured for
  your role.
- If it prints `NO_TASK`, stop waiting for work.
- If it prints `TASK: <path>`, treat the printed `PAYLOAD` as the task.
- A Git handoff payload describes workflow actions in prose; it is not itself a
  shell command. Execute only the named repository helper scripts and ordinary
  Git commands needed to perform those actions. Never infer an executable name
  from a workflow label or sentence.
- If it prints `TASK_NAME: <name>`, use that as the stable task name for any
  work you forward from that task.
- If it prints `READINESS: review-ready`, treat it only as permission for the named next review. `READINESS: qa-ready` permits the specifier to integrate only into `qa`. `READINESS: release-candidate` permits only the architect's cumulative release review and terminal gate. `READINESS: final-ready` is the only state that may advance `master`.
- If it prints `BATCH: <path>`, treat each printed `BATCH_ITEM` as part of the
  current batch in helper-delivered order.
- Use only the task information printed by the helper scripts.
- Treat a role as working only while it has a live command or a current progress
  lease for the exact task and handoff. An active report without either item is
  stale and the role is available.
- Before each next planned action at a safe boundary, renew the exact active lease
  with `node swarmforge/scripts/role-progress-lease.mjs renew-current "$PWD"`.
  Task completion clears that lease through the queue transaction.
- If a tmux wake-up arrives while observable work is active, keep ordinary mail
  queued. If no command or current lease is active, process the exact queued
  handoff even when an older role report still says active.
- The preceding idle-only rule applies to ordinary mail. A validated interrupt
  beginning `USER-AUTHORIZED UNBLOCKER` is handled after the current tool call
  returns and before another planned action. Use `unblocker_claim.sh` and
  `unblocker_complete.sh`; do not interrupt a running command destructively or
  edit nested unblocker state. Resume retains the same active handoff. Replace
  transitions only the exact active and queued replacement identities carried
  by the validated message.
- When the task or batch is fully complete, run `done_with_current.sh`.
- `note` handoffs are tasks too; after reading or acting on a note, run
  `done_with_current.sh` before accepting any other handoff.
- If `done_with_current.sh` prints `TASK: <path>`, treat the printed `PAYLOAD`
  as the next task.
- If `done_with_current.sh` prints `BATCH: <path>`, treat each printed
  `BATCH_ITEM` as part of the next batch in helper-delivered order.
- If a done helper prints `NO_TASK`, stop waiting for work.
- On restart, run `ready_for_next.sh` and follow its output.
